  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/327120">Remark <span data-tag="327120">5.3.1.10</span>.</a> An $\infty $-category $\calC $ is $\kappa $-filtered if and only if, for every $\kappa $-small partially ordered set $A$, $\calC $ has the right lifting property with respect to the inclusion $\Nerve (A) \subseteq \Nerve (A)^{\triangleright } \simeq \Nerve (A \cup \{  \infty \}  )$. The “only if” direction is obvious. For the converse, we observe that for every $\kappa $-small diagram $p: K \rightarrow \calC $, the $\infty $-category $\calC _{p/}$ is equivalent to $\calC _{q/}$, where $q$ denotes the composition $K'' \stackrel{p'}{\rightarrow } K \stackrel{p}{\rightarrow } \calC $. Here $K''$ is the second barycentric subdivision of $K$ and $p'$ is the map described in Variant <a href="/tag/49F1B7" data-tag="49F1B7">4.2.3.15</a>. We now observe that $K''$ is equivalent to the nerve of a $\kappa $-small partially ordered set. </p>
